The rapidly increasing cumulative prevalence of coronavirus disease (COVID-19) in the European Union / European Economic Area and the United Kingdom, from January 1 to March 15, 2020
The cumulative prevalence of coronavirus disease (COVID-19) cases is showing a similar trend in EU/European Economic Zone countries and the United Kingdom which confirms that the COVID-19 pandemic is increasing rapidly across all countries while at different stages depending on the country.
Depending on the experience from Italy, countries, hospitals and intensive care units should increase their preparedness for a rapid increase in COVID-19 patients who will need health care, especially intensive care.
On December 31, 2019, a group of pneumonia cases with undisputed anemia-science was reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention on 9 January 2020, China notified the novel coronavirus, which is now referred to as severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, the disease generated by SARS-CoV-2 infection has been named coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
The evidence to date is that 80% of individuals with COVID-19 have a mild disease, i.e. with pneumonia or without pneumonia, respiratory tract infections and most of them recover.
In 14% of cases, COVID-19 develops as a more serious disease requiring hospitalization, while the remaining 6% of cases have an observation of a dire disease requiring severe care.
The death rate of patients admitted to hospitals due to COVID-19 is 4%.
In this study, we assess the cumulative prevalence of COVID-19 in each country of the European Union/EU/EEA and the United Kingdom (UK) and compare it to its Hubei Province, China.
We also compare the current number of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA countries and the UK to Italy during January 31 – 15 March 2020.
Cases of COVID-19 in EU/EEA countries and the UK
After China, COVID-19 had a further geographical expansion and the current COVID-19 pandemic's actions in the rest of the world moved ahead of this country.
On 11 March 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHW) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
EuroSurveillance 2020, the first European confirmed COVID-19 cases were reported in accordance with the WHO case definition in Spiteri et al.
In the EU/EEA, the first three confirmed cases were reported by France on 24 January 2020 among those returning from Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
As of March 15, 2020, cases of COVID-19 were detected in all 30 EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom (UK), with 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths reported 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths between 31st December 2019 and that date from Italy.
Achieving Cumulative Number and Cumulative Prevalence of COVID-19 Cases
At the European Centers for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC), only official sources such as the Ministry of Health of the Countries, the National and Regional Health Authority, and every country in the world received from the WHO are calculated at 8:00 a.m.
These data were used to assess the COVID-19 trend in the EU/EEA and the UK and compare them to disease in Italy.
As a representative of the proliferation of active COVID-19 cases, we thus calculated the brief cumulative prevalence of COVID-19 cases of COVID-19 taking into account the natural course of COVID-19 in every EU/EEA country and the UK during the period of 1st January - 15 March 2020.
We also displayed the cumulative number of reported cases in each country till 8:00 a.m. on March 15, 2020, compared to Italy in the period of January 31 - March 15, 2020.
Trend of COVID-19 in EU/EEA countries and the UK
The 14-da-day-abbreviation trend of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA countries and the UK generally follows Hubei Prefecture (China) (Picture 1).
In the EU/EEA and the entire U.K., the overall cumulative prevalence of COVID-19 began to grow around February 21 and grew rapidly around 28 February 2020.
This was mainly due to the rapid increase in the number of reported cases from Italy, but all other EU/EEA countries and the UK witnessed the same increasing trend in the cumulative prevalence of COVID-19 (seductive materials).
The cumulative number of COVID-19 cases compared to EU/EEA countries in the EU/EEA countries and the U.K. in the U.D. January 31 – 15 March 2020.
It specifically shows that as of 8:00 a.m. on March 15, 15 other EU/EEA countries and the UK had already reported the total number of cases comparable to Italy's cases only 3 weeks ago or in a short time.
Our results indicate that the number of reported cases of COVID-19 is increasing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the UK.
The trend seen in the cumulative prevalence of COVID-19 suggests that the pandemic is increasing with comparative momentum in all countries.
It is in spite of having countries at different stages, variations in national public health responses, and potentially different case definitions in countries and different protocols to choose patients who must necessarily be screened for confirmation of COVID-19, including being caught and tested.
In early March 2020, physicians in the affected areas of Italy spoke about the situation in which 10% of COVID-19 patients were in need of intensive care and media sources informed that hospitals and intensive care units have already reached their maximum capacity in these areas.
The figures of COVID-19 cases admitted to the hospital and/or intensive care unit are currently only available at the EU/EEA level for 6% and 1% of cases, respectively (not shown statistics).
They, however, must be collected in a methodological manner to supplement the current monitoring data that focus on the number of reported cases and the number of deaths.
The study in 2010-11 showed a huge difference in the availability of intensive care and intermediate care beds in Europe from 29.2 to 4.2 beds per 100,000 population in Germany.
This means that countries may have more or less resources than that of Italy (12.5 intensive care and intermediate care beds per 100,000 population in 2010-11).
The prevassion of hospitalization COVID-19 cases related to 90% of the risk-related hospitalization COVID-19 cases has been provided in the sixth update of ECDC's rapid risk assessments on COVID-19 with estimates for every EU/EEA country and the U.K.
Since so far, cases have remained swarms in some areas in the EU/EEA countries and the UK, hospitals and in-depth care units generally provide services to the defined regional public, information about cases and intensive care beds should be made primarily available at the Nomenclature of Territorial Unit for Statistics 2 (NUTS-2).
The experience gained from Italy and the current trend in other countries shows that the COVID-19 pandemic is growing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the UK.
Countries, hospitals, and intensive care units should thus prepare themselves for an increase in the number of COVID-19 patients in need of constant community transmission of SARS-CoV-2 and health care, especially intensive care that is taking place in the affected areas of Italy.
As pointed out in the recent ECDC's rapid risk assessments, with the approach to mitigating by limitation, a faster, proactive and comprehensive approach is required to slow the spread of SARS-CoV-2, because if not implemented in time, it may not provide enough time to understand, acknowledge, and optimize your feedback accordingly.
The Rapid Risk Assessment also lists public health measures in addressing the impact of the pandemic.
To slow the spread of SARS-CoV-2, countries have very little opportunities to further increase their control efforts and reduce the pressure on health care.
Having failed, it is very likely that health care regimes of other EU/EEA countries will face a rapid increase in patients requiring intensive care in the coming days or weeks.
More than 3,000 people have been killed so far due to Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) outbreak of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and more than 80,000 people have been infected elsewhere in China and the world, resulting in a disaster for humans.
Like its homogeneous virus SARS-CoV, which led to thousands of people in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 can also be transmitted from bats and produces the same symptoms by the good mechanism.
Although COVID-19 has a low severity and mortality compared to SARS, it is much more transitive and affects men more than that of elderly people and women.
In response to the rapidly increasing number of publications on emerging diseases, this article strives to provide a topical and comprehensive review on the rapidly evolving research topic.
We will include fundamentals on epidemiology, forology, virology, diagnostics, treatment, forecasting and prevention.
Although there are still a lot of questions to find answers, we hope this review will help in understanding and eradicating the deadly disease.
Vasantotsav on January 25, 2020 has becomes an unexpected and unforgettable memory for all the Chinese people who were requested to stay at home for the whole holiday and for several weeks thereafter due to the outbreak of Novel Viral Disease.
The virus is very similar to the coronavirus (CoV) that led to an outbreak of ultra acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003; therefore, on February 11, 2020, it was named SARS-CoV-2 by the World Health Organization (WHO) and the related disease was named CoV-19 (COVID-19).
The pandemic originated from Wuhan, China and rapidly spread to about 50 other countries across the country and the world.
As of March 2, 2020, there have been more than 80,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19 with more than 40,000 patients discharged from the hospital and more than 3,000 patients died due to the virus.
The WHO has warned that COVID-19 is “enemie number 1” of the public and is possibly more powerful than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih/pubmed/), more than more than 200 articles have been published on COVID-19 including virus-science, epidemiology, formidemiology, formidemiology, diagnosis, and treatment, in less than two months since the first report on 7 January 2020, setting a sequence of the virus separately from many patients.
This review seeks to present the essence of research progress on a new and rapidly evolving topic area.
Whenever it is possible, we will try to compare COVID-19 with other diseases caused by SARS and CoV, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (outbreak in MERS, 2012).
We will also discuss what we have learned so far so far regarding the prevention and prediction of the disease as well as some other necessary questions.
COVs have traditionally considered non-lethal pathogens for humans with producing about 15% of the common Cold.
However, in this century we have encountered highly pathogenic human CoVs twice, i.e., SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which were originally seen outbreaks in China in 2003 and in 2012 in Saudi Arabias, respectively, and soon dispersed to many other countries with terrible malaise and mortality.
Thus, current COVID-19 is the third CoV outbreak in the recorded history of humans.
As shown in Figure 1,1, a herd of pneumonia, whose origin was unknown, was first reported to the Chinese National Health Commission from Wuhan on December 31, 2019.
Seven days later the sequence of CoV was released.
On 15 January 20, the first deadly case was reported from Wuhan.
During this time, the epidemic spread rapidly to nearby cities, provinces and countries.
On January 20, infection was reported in health care providers, which indicated that human to human transmission was possible.
On January 23, the city of Wuhan was under a lockdown with all its public traffic closed.
On January 24, an earlier clinical study on the disease informed that out of 41 patients with rectified cases, only 21 had direct contact with the seafood market in Wuhan, which was believed to be the beginning site of infection from an unknown animal source.
On January 30, the WHO declared the outbreak a global health disaster.
By the time of this report, the disease has already spread across China and about 50 other countries around the world (Fig.
As the situation is increasing rapidly, the final prevalence and intensity of the outbreak is yet to be determined.
The multi-central study of 8,866 patients, including 4,021 confirmed COVID-19 patients on 11th February 2020, presented a more-to-dated depiction of the pandemic (https://mp.weixin.qqq.com/s/UlBi-HX_r HPX_rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 infected people of all ages, but mainly 30-65 years of age.
About half of the infected persons (47.7%) were over 50 years of age, very little less than 20 years and only 14 infected persons were less than 10 years of age.
SARS-CoV-2 became more infected than female (0.27/100,000) (0.31/100,000).
COVID-19 spread mainly in and around Hubei into swarms.
COVID-19 took an average of 5 (2-9) days from start-to-diagnosis.
The average incubation period was 4.8 (3.0-7.2).
The an average time (4.8-13) was 9.5 (4.8-13).
The original fertility number (R0) was 3.77 (95% CI:3.51-4.05), and the adjusted R0 was 2.23-4.82.
The number of people infected increased sharply before 23 January 2020, which corresponds to widespread traffic ahead of the Spring Festival in China.
The mortality rate of patients with confirmed cases was 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10-1.86%) and the adjusted mortality rate of all patients was 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02-4.59%).
The three major risk factors for COVID-19 were gender (male), ≥60, and severe pneumonia.
Covs are sub-families of single-lading large and corravited viruses of RNA.
They can be divided into four generations, i.e. alpha, beta, gamma and delta of which alpha and beta-CoVs are known to infect humans.
The casing nozzle (S) glycoprotein, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV connects to its cellular secret angiotensin-changing enzyme 2 (ACE2) and dipeptidyl peptidase 4 (DPP4), respectively, and then the membrane is merged.
The viral RNA is released into the genome cellular fluid; after replication of the viral genome, the casing forms genomeic RNA virion vellicles with glycoproteins and nucleopacsid proteins, which later merges into the plasma membrane for the release of the virus.
The first genometic sequence of SARS-CoV-2 was reported on 10 January 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 was found to be a new variant of beta-CoV, which found more than 99.98% genetic similarity in 10 indexed samples collected from the Huanon seafood market in Wuhan.
SARS-CoV-2 is genetically more similar to SARS-CoV compared to MERS-CoV.
The SARS-CoV-2 particle was found in the ultra-fine segments of the human aerobic spheriology.
Human ACE2 was found to be available for SARS-CoV, along with SARS-CoV-2.
However, the S protein of SARS-CoV-2 connects to human ACE2 with a greater weakness than SARS-CoV which corresponds to the fact that SARS-CoV-2 produces less severe infections in patients than SARS-CO.
SARS-CoV-2 can also create secreted proteins coded by novel short proteins and orf8 coded by orf3b.
Orf3b of SARS-CoV-2 can play a role in viral pathogenicity and block the expression of IFNβ; however orf8 does not have any known functional domain or motif.
On 18 February 2020, Zhou, et al. reported the cryo-EM structure of full-length human ACE2 at 2.9 Å resolution in the mixture with the amino acid conductor B0AT1.
He found that the mixture, in which there were free and closed compositions, was compiled as a dimer and the ACE2-B0AT1 mixture could add two S proteins that provide evidence for the identification and infection of the CoV.
B0AT1 can become a medical goal for drug screening to suppress SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Basic & Intermediate Nutrients
It is known that both SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV originated from bats and were transmitted in humans by civet cats and camels, respectively.
By ractive comparisons with other COVCs of SARS-CoV-2, bats were considered the basic nutrient of SARS-CoV-2 because the new virus is 96% equal to two SARS-like COVs called Bat-SL-CoVZX45 and Bat-SL-CoVZX21.
However, which intermediate nutrient has been unknowingly assisted in inculciating humans by crossing the virus species barrier and the transmission route is yet to be clarified.
Ji, et al., proposed the snake as a conductor of the virus from bats to humans that included similar recombination with S proteins.
According to a study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, indicated that pangolins, often used in traditional Chinese medicine with long muzzles, ant-eating mammals SARS-CoV-2 and POO found in pangolins are a potential intermediate nutrient of SARS-CoV-2 based on 99% genetic parity.
However, the dissemination of a difference of 1% in both the genomes is still a big difference; therefore, conclusive results are awaited for concrete evidence (Figure (Figure 33).
The physical properties of SARS-CoV-2 are mainly not yet known.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can live up to 5 days at less than 48 hours and 20°C and 40%-50% humidity in artificial ambient dry environment.
SARS-CoV-2 can have the same properties.
It has been reported that SARS-CoV-2 is sensitive to 30 minutes at 56 °C and ultraviolet rays; Ether, 75% ethanol, chlorinated disinfectants, perasustic acids, chlorotroforms and other fat solvents can effectively inactivate the virus, but not chlorhexidine.
The whole human population generally lacks resistance to SARS-CoV-2 and is, therefore susceptible to the novel virus.
At present, no detailed study has been communicated regarding immunological response to SARS-CoV-2.
Therefore, we can only refer to previous studies on other CoVs, specifically SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (Figure 4.4).
Generally, after the virus penetrates the nutrient, it is first identified by the endogenous immune system of the nutrutin from the nutrient identification receptors (PRRs), including C-type lectin-like receptors (TLRs), toll-like receptors (TLRs), NOD-like receptors (NLRs).
By various pathways, the virus induces the manifestation of inferration factors, the maturation of dendritic cells and the synthesis of type I interferron (IFNs) that limits the proliferation of the virus and accelerating the macrophage fegocytosis of viral antigens.
However, the N protein of SARS-CoV can assist the virus in avoiding immune responses.
Soon, the optimized immune response gets involved in fighting the virus.
T lymphocytes, including CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, play an important role in defense.
CD4+ T cells stimulate B cells to produce virus-specific conservators and CD8+ T cells directly kill virus-infected cells.
T auxiliary cells produce pro-inflammatory cytokines to assist protecting cells.
However, CoV can block T cell functions by killing T cells.
Tridoshan resistivity, including supplements like C3a and C3a and C5a and antibodies, is also necessary to fight viral infections.
For example, inservated immune from the recovered patient deactivated MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, the immune system's excessive response locally generate a very large number of free radicals that can also cause severe damage to the lungs and other organs, and multi-organ failure and death in the worst conditions.
The SARS-CoV-2 infection, which is characterized by the onset in the herd, is more likely to affect older people and pregnant women with comorbidities.
It is normal that people who come into contact with a higher number of viruses or whose immune functions are mixed are more likely to be infected than others.
Depending on the first 425-case studies in Wuhan, an estimated average incubation period of SARS-CoV-2 is 1-14 days, mostly 3-7 days.
However, the 1,099 cases study observed that the incubation period was an average of 3 days and its range was 0 to 24 days.
As mentioned above, a recent study based on the demography of 8,866 cases showed that the incubation period was 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days.
It is very important for health authorities to accommodate effective quarantine times based on the most accurate incubation period and thus preventing the virus from transmittincting the virus of infected but asymptomatic people.
As a normal practice, people who come in contact with or infected with the virus usually require a 14-da-day quarantine.
Should the quarantine time be extended to 24 days?
Fequerr is often the leading and early symptoms of COVID-19 with no symptoms such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscle pain, dizziness, headache, sore throat, nascarriage, chest pain, diarrhea, nausea, and vomiting.
Some patients experienced breathing and/or hypoxamemia a week after the onset of the disease.
In severe cases, patients rapidly increased the development of acute respiratory syndrome, septic shock, metabolic acidity and cogulopathy.
Patients with acute fever without fever and/or respiragnum and pulmonary depiction abnormalities should be screened for the virus for premature diagnosis.
Demographic studies at the end of December 2019 observed that the percentage of symptoms was 98% for fever, 76% for dry cough, 55% for breathing and 3% for diarrhea; 8% of patients needed the support of ventilation.
Similar findings were communicated in two recent studies of herds generated by transmission from family herds and symptomless individuals.
Comparatively, the 2012 demographic study observed that MERS-CoV patients also had fever (98%), dry cough (47%) and breathing discomfort (55%).
However, more than COVID-19 patients, 80% of them required ventilation support and this is consistent with the more lethality of MERS than COVID-19.
Diarrhoea (26%) and sore throat (21%) were also seen in patients with MERS.
SARS patients have observed that fever (99%-100%), dry cough (29%-75%), breathing discomfort (40%-42%), diarrhea (20-25%) and sore throat (13-25%) were the major symptoms and about 14%-20% of patients needed ventilation support.
The COVID-19 fatality rate was 2% as of February 14, when confirmed cases across the world had reached 66,576.
Comparatively, as of November 202, the mortality rate of SARS was 8,096 of confirmed cases.
For MERS, the mortality rate was 2,494, based on a demographic study from June 2012, 37% of confirmed cases.
The earlier study reported that the RSS-CoV-2 was 6.47 with a 95% trust interval (CI) of 5.71-7.23 while the R0 of SARS-CoV was only between 2 and 4.
Compared to SARS-CoV-2 in relation to symptoms, mortality and R0, SARS-CoV-2 is presented in Table 1.11.
The above-mentioned statistics indicate that SARS-CoV-2's ability to spread is greater than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but it's less lethal than both later.
Therefore, it is more challenging to control the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic than MERS-COV and SARS-CoV.
The herd usually begins with the same family or the same group of vehicles such as a cruise veins.
Patients often have a history of travel or residence or contact with infected persons or infected persons or patients in Wuhan or other affected areas within the last two weeks from the onset.
However, it has been informed that people can convest the virus without symptoms for more than two weeks and patients who have been discharged from the hospital can convect the virus again, which warns an extension of quarantine time.
The number of peripheral white blood cells (especially lymphatic cells) in patients at an early stage is normal or reduced.
For example, lymphopania and increased aspartate aminotransferess levels and increased aspartate aminotransferess levels and viralitis were found in 1,099 COVID-19 patients, including Lymphica cell calculation &lt; 1×109/L, including 1×109/L.
Some patients had increased levels of liver and muscle enzymes and myoglobins in the blood of most patients increased C-reactive protein and erythrocyte sedemination in the blood.
In patients with severe cases, a product of fibrin collapse present in the blood, D-dimmer levels, had increased and lymphatic cell calculations continued to decrease.
Abnormalities are found in chest radiography in most COVID-19 patients and represented by bilateral macular images in the lung or ground glass ambiguity.
Patients usually develop abnormal pneumonia, acute lung damage and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
Whe are severely compromised by gas exchange due to unruly incurability, fluid accumulation and progressive fibrosis.
Type-I and Type-II pneumosite's reactivates the reactivating level and increases surface tension and thus reduces the ability of the lung bloasting and increases the risk of stopping the functioning of the lungs.
Therefore, the poorest conclusions of chest radiography often remain parallel with the most serious condition of the disease.
On 18 February 202, the first disease-diagnosis analysis on COVID-19 saw the pneumocite in the lungs of a pneumocite, the formation of the helian membrane and the porous leakage of the lymph cells, and the multiple-nogenetic cells that corresponded to the pathology and ARDS of viral infections and were similar to SARS and MERS patients.
Detecting SARS-CoV-2 R.N.A. by reverse-transcriptase polymerase chain response (RT-PCR) was used as the leading criteria for diagnosing COVID-19.
However, due to the high incorrect-negative rate, which could accelerate the epidemic, began to be used to diagnose clinical manifestations in China on 13 February 2020 (which was no longer just dependent on RT-PCR).
The same condition also occurred with the diagnosis of SARS.
It is, therefore, necessary and necessary for effective diagnosis of history, clinical manifestations, laboratory examinations and radiological findings.
On February 14, 2020, the Feng Zheng Group reported a protocol using the CRISPR-based SHERLOCK technology to detect SARS-CoV-2 that, using dipsticks, detects artificial SARS-CoV-2 RNA segments at 20 × 10-18 mol/L to 200 × 10-18 mol/L (10-100 copies per microliter per microliter of input), using a dipstick.
If it is verified in diagnostic samples, it is expected that the new technology can dramatically increase sensitivity and convenience.
Due to the lack of experience with Novel COVO, physicians can provide mainly supportive care to COVID-19 patients with previously been used for the treatment of other CoVOs such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV and other viral diseases (Talika (2.2).
These treatments include current and possible treatment with antiviral drugs, immunosuppressives, steroids, plasma from recovered patients, Chinese medicine and psychological assistance.
Plasmas from the recovered patients was also proposed for use in the treatment.
There is a competition in pharmaceutical companies to develop antibodies and vaccines against the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 mainly invades the lungs at the beginning and possibly even on other organs in a lower range, which ACE2 expresses such as gastrointestinal systems and kidneys.
Nevertheless, respirational dysfunction and failure remain a major threat to the patients and are the leading causes of death.
Therefore, respiratory support is important to get rid of symptoms and save lives and it includes general oxygen therapy, high-flow oxygen, non-entry ventilation and entrenched mechanical ventilation, depending on the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms have to be assisted by extracorporeal membrane oxidation (ECMO), modified heart-pulmonary sypath techniques used in the treatment of life fatal heart or respiration failure.
At the same time, maintaining electrolyte balance, prevention and treatment of secondary infections and septic shock, and protecting the functions of vital organs is also essential for SARS-CoV-2 patients.
It is known that cytokine storm occurs as a result of an excessive response of the immune system in SARS and MERS patients.
Cytokine sterms TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IL-6, IFNβ, IFNβ, IFNγ, and MCP-1 are a type of systemic response characterized by the release of cytokines.
These cytokines provoke immune cells to release a large number of free radicals that are the leading causes of ARDS and multiple organ failure.
Immunisforming is necessary in the treatment of cytokine storms, especially in severe patients.
Corticosteroids and tocilizumab, IL6-anti-moclonal immunctivil are used for the treatment of cytokine storm.
Other immunitative treatments for cytokinin storm include changes in T cell guided immune response; IFN-γ, IL-1, and blockage of TNF; JAK blocking; blinatumomabe; suppressive of cytokine signaling 4; and HDAC inhibitors.
As an immune repressive steroid, steroids were widely used in the treatment of SARS to reduce the seriousness of inflammatory damage.
However, high doses of steroids were not useful in severe lung damage in SARS and COVID-19 patients.
Instead, they can cause serious side effects while dramatically affecting the disease-diasmination, especially non-vascular fogulation.
Nevertheless, curticosteroid short-term procedures on low to medium doses have been recommended to be carefully used in critically ill COVID-19 patients.
Till the time of writing, no effective antiviral therapy has been confirmed.
Remdesivir, a nucleotide analogue being given into the vein, has been found to be effective in an U.S. patient of COVID-19.
Remdesivir is the novel antiviral drug developed by Gilead for the treatment of diseases caused by Ebola and Malburg viruses initially.
Later, Remdesivir also showed a possible inhibition of other monogamous RNA viruses, including MERS and SARS viruses.
Depending on these, Gilead has provided compounds to China to carry out two tests on persons infected with SARS-CoV-2 and the results are quite expected.
At the same time, baricitinibi, interferon-αααααα, and ribavirin have been suggested as a potential therapy for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, liver damage and other adverse reactions may occur after therapy with iopinavir/retonavir.
These treatments should be carefully monitored along with other drugs used in patients.
Plasma and anti-immunity production from recovered patients
Other patients suffering from the same disease have a long-term history of blood aggregation from patients who have been cured from an infectious disease in order to treat other patients with the same disease or to prevent healthy persons from being diagnosed.
In fact, the blood of the recovered patients has a high level of antibodies relative to the pathogrative.
Immunoglobuline (Ig). The antibodies produced by B lymphatic cells to fight pathogens and other external substances are immunoglobulin (Ig) and they identify unique molecules in the pathogens and deactivate them directly.
Depending on this, plasma was collected from the blood of such a group of patients who had recovered from COVID-19 and was interresponsed in 10 critically ill patients.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours, with low growth and viral weight and better oxygen saturatization in the blood.
However, validation and clarification is necessary to propose the methodology to be used on a large scale until specific treatments are developed.
At the same time, despite therapeutic effects, certain disadvantages related to plasma should be carefully considered.
For example, antibades can over-stimulate the immune response and generate cytokine discharge syndrome, which is potentially life-threatening toxicity.
Concentral concentration of antibodies in the blood is generally low, and the demand for plasma is high to treat critically ill patients.
It's harder to develop and produce so fast specific imitators to fight the global pandemic.
Therefore, it is more important and behavioral to isolating B cells from recovered patients and recognizing the genetic codes that code effective antibodies or examining effective immunities towards the essential proteins of the virus.
Thus, we can rapidly increase the production of antibodies.
TCM has been used for thousands of years to treat a variety of diseases in China.
However, its effects depend on a combination of different components primarily in a different formula depending on the diagnosis of a prognosis based on the TCM principles.
Most effective components are yet unknown or meaningless because it is difficult to remove and verify such components or their optimal combination.
At present, TCM has become one of the major alternative therapies for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or patients who have recovered from severe stages due to lack of effective and specific therapy for COVID-19.
For e.m., Shoe Feng Ji Doo capsules and Lien Hua Qiving Wayne capsules were found to be effective for the treatment of COVID-19.
87% of patients using TCM, including Gunsu (63.7%), Ninxia (50%), and Hunan (50%) saw the highest recovery rates in the treatment of COVID-19 in several provinces of China, while Hubei province where only about 30% of COVID-19 patients received TCM use, the recovery rate was a minimum (13%).
However, this is quite a thick comparison because many other impact factors, such as the number of patients and severity, should be included in the assessment.
On February 18, 2020, Zheng and co-workers only published the study to compare Western medicine (WM) treatment and the combined treatment of WM and TCM.
They found that the WM+TCM group had a significantly lower time than only the W.M.+TCM group requiring a normal body temperature to normal, symptoms and hospitalization.
The most influential, the symptomatic dreal rate of the WM+TCM group than the W.M+TCM group was remarkably low (lightly severe) (7.4% vs. 46.2%) and the mortality rate was lower in the WM+TCM group only than the WM group (8.8% vs 39%).
Nevertheless, for the efficacy and safety of the TCM is still looking forward to more controlled trials on a large scale and in more centers.
It will also be interesting to mark the system of actions and clarify the effective components of TCM treatments or their combinations if possible.
Suspicious or confirmed patients of COVID-19 experience a lot of fear from highly contagious and deadly disease and those quarantined also experience dullness, loneliness, and anger.
In addition, symptoms of infection such as fever, low-oxidancy, and cough, as well as the adverse effects of treatment such as insomnia produced by corticosteroids, can cause more discomfort and mental suffering.
In the early stages of the SARS outbreak, several psychiatrological pathologies, including constant stress, restlessness, nervous attacks, psychiatric enthusiasm, mental symptoms, deliriument, even suicide were reported.
Finding mandatory contacts and quarantine as part of public health responses to the COVID-19 outburst can make it more restless and glutant about the effects of stigmatism on people, quarantine, and their families and friends.
Therefore, mental health care should be provided to COVID-19 patients, suspicious persons and persons who came in contact with them, as well as the general public.
Psychiatric support should include the establishment of multidisciplinary mental health teams, the use of professional electronic instruments and applications to avoid clear communication and close contact with each other with constant and accurate updates about SARS-CoV-2 outbreaks and treatment plans.
Effective vaccines are necessary to disrupt the chain of transmission from animal cells and sensitive nutrients from infected humans and are often complementary to antiviral treatment under the control of the epidemic due to increasing viruses.
Efforts have been made to develop S protein-based vaccines to generate long-term and powerful inactivating antibodies and/or defensive immunity towards SARS-CoV.
Livable-dignated vaccines have been evaluated in fauna paradigms of SARS.
However, these Vivo efficacy of these candidate vaccines in elderly people and deadly challenging patterns and their protection against zoonotic virus infection is yet to be determined before the start of any clinical studies.
This is probably the reason because SARS gradually expired 17 years ago and no new case has been reported since then.
Conversely due to the presence of zoonotic sources in the regions of MERS sporadic cases and herds continue to originate in the Middle East and spread to other areas.
Vaccination strategies have been developed for MERS using sub-units of inactive viruses, DNA plasmids, viral vectors, nanoparticles, virus-like particles and reconvent proteins and some have been evaluated in animal patterns.
For non-resitable individuals to develop a safe and effective vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 is an urgent and important task to control the ongoing pandemic.
However, it is challenging to overcome the difficulty due to the dynamic variations of a long-time requirement (average 18 months) in the development of the vaccine (an average 18 months).
As a new disease, COVID-19 has recently started revealing its entire diagnostic procedural in thousands of patients.
In most cases, the patient can recover gradually without the symptoms of other diseases.
However, like SARS and MERS, COVID-19 is also related to high morbidity and mortality in patients with severe cases.
Therefore, it is necessary for health-care agencies to create a disease-diagnosis model to prioritize their services, especially in areas with limited resources.
Depending on yet-informed clinical studies, the following factors can affect or relate to the diagnosis of COVID-19 patients (Taule (Table 33):
Age: Age was the most important factor for the disease-diagnosis of SARS, which is also true for COVID-19.
The 8,866 cases study of COVID-19 occurred mainly at the age of 30-65, of which 47.7% of patients were over the age of 50, as described above.
Patients in need of intensive care were more likely to have underlying comorbidities and complexities and who didn't have such a need. They were remarkably older (at the medical age of 51 versus 66), which indicates the age as the prognosity factor of COVID-19 patients.
Gender: SARS-CoV-2 has been infected more men than women (0.31/100,000 versus 0.27/100,000), as mentioned above.
Co-morbidities and Complications: COVID-19 patients who need intensive care are more likely to suffer from acute heart damage and arrhythmias.
Heart events were also the main cause of death in SARS patients.
It has been reported that SARS-CoV-2 may also be associated with ACE2-positive colengiosites that can cause liver disease in COVID-19 patients.
It is worth noting that there is deep correlation between age and the underlying disease and can interfere with each other.
Unusual laboratory findings: The level of C-reactive protein (CRP) in the blood shows the severity of inflammation or tissue damage and has been proposed as a possible prognosis factor of disease, response to therapy and finally recovery.
Correlation of CRP level has also been proposed due to the severity of COVID-19 and diagnosis.
At the same time, enlarged lactate hydrogenase (LDH), aspartate aminotransfares (AST), alenine aminotransfares (ALT) and creatinine kines (CK) can also assy to estimate the results.
These enzymes are widely manifested in multiple organs, especially in the heart and liver, and are released during tissue damage.
Thus, they are traditional indicators of heart or liver diseases.
Prominent clinical symptoms: Among other problems should be considered to precognize COVID-19 results and complications of COVID-19 of topical progressions of chest radiography and clinical symptoms.
The use of steroids: As mentioned above, steroids are immunosuppressive commonly used as convenating therapy in infectious diseases to reduce the severity of steroidal damage.
Since high doses of corticosteroid were widely used in severe SARS patients, many survivors suffered from non-vascular ostegulation with a lifetime disability and poor quality of life.
Therefore, if necessary, steroids should be used in low doses and COVID-19 patients for a short term.
Mental Stress: As mentioned above, many patients have suffered from extraordinary stress during the COVID-19 outburst, as they often endured a long-term and extreme uncertainty of quarantine and witnessed the death of close family members and fellow patients.
It is very important to provide psychological advice and long-term support to help these people come out of stress and return to normal life.
According to demographic studies so far so far, epidemiological characteristics of COVID-19 appear different from SARS.
As well as relinaculating in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 can efficiently relaminate the upper respiratory tract and does not cause any symptoms at an early stage of an infection like other Coctory-producing Cots.
Therefore, infected patients can generate a large amount of the virus during daily activities in the initial stage or incubation period with causing too much difficulty in the control of the epidemic.
However, the transmission of SARS-CoV was believed to have occurred when the patients were too sick, while most transmissions did not take place during the early stages.
Thus, the current outbreak of COVID-19 is much more serious and difficult to control by SARS outbreaks.
Very major efforts are currently underway in China, including lockdowns in Wuhan and surrounding cities and the constant quarantine of almost the entire population due to the hope of disrupting the transmission of SARS-CoV-2.
Although these steps are dramatically destroying the economy and other regions of the country, the number of new patients is decreasing, which points to the ups of the pandemic.
The most optimistic estimate is that the outbreak will end by the end of March and will last for 3-4 months.
However, some other experts are not so optimistic.
Paul Hunter, et al., estimated that COVID-19 which appears far more contagious than SARS will not end in 2020.
Ira Longini, et al., established the pattern to predict the outcome of the pandemic and indicated that SARS-CoV-2 could infect two-thirds of the world's population.
Canada's group reported that SARS-CoV-2 has been identified in both mid-turbinet and throat swab of patients who went from the hospital 2 weeks ago which indicates that the identified new virus can become a frequent case like influenza.
However, promising signals have been received from China based on the diminishing number of new cases, which indicate that current strategies may be working.
Initially, Ebola was predicted to have one-million cases with half a million of deaths.
However, by strict quarantine and isolation, the disease has been finally controlled.
It is possible that like SARS-CoV-2 infectivity weakens and eventually eliminates or becomes a less pathogenic virus of coexisting with humans.
The COVID-19 pandemic comparison with SARS and MERS is given below (Figure (Figure 55).
SARS-CoV-2 is also highly transmissible by coughing or sneezing and possibly direct contact with materials contaminated with the virus.
The virus was also found in feces, which also generates a new possibility of oral transmission from stools.
A recent study of 138 cases informed that 41% of cases, including 17 patients already with other diseases and 40 health care providers, were possibly due to medical infections.
Therefore, great care should also be taken to protect the people or infected people standing next or infected in contact with humans, especially health care providers, social workers, family members, colleagues, and patients.
The first line of defense that can be used to reduce the risk of infection is to wear a mask; the use of both surgical masks and N95 respiratory masks (series #1860s) helps in controlling the spread of the virus.
Surgical facial masks prevent dropts of fluid from a potentially infected person from going into the air or sticking to the surface of things from which they can move to others.
However, only N95 (Series #1860s) masks, with only 5% of Virian being fully exceeded, can only prevent Virians as small as 10 to 80 nms from inhaling; SARS-CoV-2 is similar to SARS-CoV in size and both are about 85 nm.
Since particles can also cross five surgical masks placed one above one, health care providers with direct contact with patients should necessarily wear N95 (series # 1860s) masks, but not surgical masks.
Along with masks, health care providers should wear isolation gowns that fit to further reduce contact with viruses.
The virus can also infect a person with the eyes.
On 22 January 2020, a physician was infected with SARS-CoV-2, although she was wearing an N95 mask; the virus may have penetrated inside the body from her devastating eyes.
Therefore, health care providers should also wear transparent facial armor or glasses while working with patients.
The general public in the affected or potentially affected areas is very strongly suggested that everyone wash their hands with disinfectant soap more often than usual, striving to stay indoor for self-quarantine and possibly limit contact with infected persons.
Three feet of proper distance is considered for people to stay away from the patient.
These activities are effective measures to reduce the risk of infection as well as to prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 came as a new virus for the human world, as reported on 7 January 2020, due to excessive similarity to SARS-CoV it would have made China highly wary based on the deep memory of SARS outbreaks in 2003.
However, as of 19 January 2020, the director of Wuhan's Center for Disease Colve consoled the citizens by saying that the novel virus has reduced human-to-human infection and limited fertility and there is no problem in preventing and limiting the disease.
This message freed the public quite fear, especially when the whole of the country was preparing for the Vasantotsav and it took a critical time to limit the disease to the lowest level in Wuhan.
Disease control agencies in China can learn bigger than this and can make significant improvements in the future.
For example, these agencies must be quite careful when making (1) public announcements because every term matters to citizens and may change their attitudes and decisions; ′ (2) should remain more sensitive and responsive to unusual information from clinics instead of waiting for formal information from physicians or authorities; (3) should be more restrictive to limit the potential epidemic to the initial stage rather than an effort to console the public; and (4) raise public awareness about the pandemic.
The COVID-19 outbreak generated by the novel virus SARS-CoV-2 began at the end of December 2019.
In less than two months, it has spread throughout China and around 50 other countries of the world by the time of this writing.
Since the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and the symptoms are also the same between COVID-19 and SARS, the COVID-19 outbreak has led to the realization of the frequency of SARS.
However, there are some notable differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are necessary to limit the pandemic and treat patients.
COVID-19 affects men more than older people and women, and the seriousness and mortality rate in the elderly is also higher than youth.
The mortality rate of SARS exceeds COVID-19 (10.91% vs 1.4.4%).
COVID-19 patients transmit the virus even if they are symptomless, while SARS patients often do when they are seriously ill, which causes a lot more difficulty in limiting the spread of COVID-19 than SARS.
It partly explains why SARS-CoV-2 spreads with faster and wider than SARS-CoV.
Regular RAN tests for SARS-CoV-2 can be negative in some COVID-19 patients.
On the other hand, the recovered patients can be re-positive for the virus.
These findings dramatically increase the risk of the spread of the virus.
Despite such rapid progress in research on COVID-19, many serious cases are yet to be resolved, which are as follows:
Where did the SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Although 96% genetic similarity was found between SARS-CoV-2 and two bat SARS-like CoVs, we still can't come to the conclusion that SARS-CoV-2 comes from bats.
Original nutrient, let's say which animal was the intermediate species in the transmission of the virus from bats into humans?
Without finding an answer to #1 and 2, we can't effectively stop broadcasting and the outbreak can never be repeated.
Although molecular impersonation and bio-chemical tests have seen that SARS-CoV-2 joins ACE2, how the virus enters the airway cells and then conducts pathological changes?
Does the virus also tying ACE2-expensive cells to other organs?
Without a clear answer to these questions, we cannot achieve fast and accurate diagnosis and effective treatment.
How long will the pandemic last?
How is the virus developing genetically during transmission in humans?
Will it become a global pandemic, gradually ending up like SARS, or will it continue to happen again and again, like the flu?
It is necessary, but it may take some time to find answers to the above and many other questions.
However, whatever the price has to be paid, we have no other option but to prevent the pandemic as soon as possible and bring our lives back to normal.
Origin of Human Coronaviruses
Due to mutuation and adaptation, coronavirus (CoVs) and their nutruments (including humans) have evolved simultaneously over a period of thousands of years.
Before 2003, there were two human CoVs (HCoVs) causing mild diseases such as the colds.
Severy acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) outperformed the situation, showcasing the devastating and deadly nature of HCoV infection.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 in central China at the end of 2019 has brought COVs back to light and its sis the more contagious potential but decreased pathogenesity than SARS-CoV.
HCoV infection is an aging disease and it will be useful for us to understand the aging origin of HCoVs.
Most HCoVs originated from bats, in which they are non-diseparated.
There's also information about the intermediate cell nutrients of some HCoVs.
The identification of animal nutrients in the prevention of human diseases has direct implications.
Execlusing the interactions of the CoV-nutrients in animals can also put important insights on the pathogenisity of CoV in humans.
In this review, we present an overview of the current information about seven HCoVs while paying attention to the history of discovery, as well as the original and interracial transmission.
Significantly, we compare and vary various HCoVs from the perspective of the gradual development and gene recombination of viruses.
The current CoV disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic is discussed in this perspective.
At the same time, the need to successfully change the nutrients and the implications of the gradual development of the virus on the severity of the disease has also been highlighted.
Coronavirus (CoVs) are from a corona virus family that includes groups of invaled, positive-sensed, single-strended RNA viruses.
These viruses sheltering the largest genome of 26 to 32 kilobase in RNA viruses were named “CoVs” due to the crown-gaiety morphology below the electron microscope.
Structurally, CoVs have non-segmented genomes that have the same structure.
The nearly two-third genome consists of two large overlapping reading frames (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are converted into pp1a and pp1ab repliquez polyproteins.
Polyprotein is further processed to generate 16 non-structural proteins named nsp1~16.
The rest of the genome consists of ORF for structural proteins, including Spike (S), Envelope (E), Membrane (M) and nucleoprotein (N).
Many pedigree-specific auxiliary proteins are also coded by various genealogical ligaments of CoVs.
Depending on the variation in protein sequences, CoV is divided into four generations (alpha-CoV, beta-CoV, gamma-CoV, and delta-CoV), of which the beta-CoV generation are mostly HCoV and sub-divisioned in four genearies (A, B, C, and D).
Ratistic evidence has shown that bats and moots are gene sources of mostly alpha-CoVs and beta-CoVs, while birds are the dominant tors of gamma-CoVs and delta-CoVs.
For thousands of years, CoVs have consistently exceeded species barriers and have emerged as some important human pathogens.
To date, seven human CoVs (HCoVs) are known.
Among them are HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 alpha-CoV.
The other five beta-CoVs include HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome coronavirus (SARS-CoV), Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63 usually cause mild symptoms like cold and/or diarrhea.
In contrast, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and newly identified SARS-CoV-2 are highly pathogatonic while causing an infection of severe lower respiratory tract in relatively more patients with a greater chance of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and hyperfunal manifestations.
The first HCoV-229E breed, B814 was separated from the nasal flow of patients with colds in the mid-1960's.
Since then, more information has been collected by detailed studies about HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, both of which produce self-limiting symptoms.
In fact, the concept was widely accepted before the SARS outburst that the infection of HCoVs is generally harmless.
The SARS outbreak in 2003, infecting more thas more than 8000 people with an untreated case fatality rate of about 10%, is one of the most devastating outbreaks in current history.
Ten years later, the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) outbreak caused a persistent epidemic in the Arabian Peninsula with sporadic spread elsewhere in the world.
The 2019 Novel HCO (2019-nCoV), which is later named SARS-CoV-2, is the causative agent of the ongoing pandemic of the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), which has led to 3,120 deaths as of March 3, 2020 and more than 91,000 people have been infected.
The warning bell is ringing and the world has to be prepared for the upcoming pandemic of SARS-CoV-2.
All seven HCoVs have the origin of bats, rats or domestic animals.
Many evidence supports the gradual evolution from the bats of all HCoVs, in which viruses are well adapted and non-displaced, but showing highly genetic variation.
The COVID-19 pandemic has presented huge therapeutic, scientific, social and ethical challenges for China and the world.
Detecting the azoological source of HCoVs provides the framework to understand the natural history, motivating force and bonding factors of the species crossing.
It can guide or make it accessible in the pursuit of SARS-CoV-2 shells, intermediates, and growing nutrients of SARS-CoV-2 with a significant impact in preventing future prevalence.
In this review, we present an overview of the aging original, intracial transmission and pathogenicity of HCoVs.
Specifically, we highlight the common topic and discuss that HCoV's ancestral viruses are non-pathogenic in their naptural nutrients, but become pathogenic after intranial transmission into new nutrients.
We will also review the trend of successive development of HCoV including an increase in contagiativity, often accompanied by a reduction in pathogenicity.
In this perspective, the outcome of the ongoing SARS-CoV-2 outbreak is also discussed.
The information of animal CoVs is from the end of the 1930s.
Before the HCoV-229E breed, the first-injected HCoV-229E breed of patients with an infected common cold was separated from many infected animals, including turkeys, rats, cows, pigs, cats and dogs.
Seven HCoVs have been identified in the last decades.
A brief summary of the chronology of the history of the discovery of HCoV (Table 1) will be informative and instructive.
HCoV-229E's first-bready was separated from the respiratory tract of patients with upper respiratory tract infections in the year 1966, which later became adapted to growing in WI-38 lung cellular lines.
Patients infected with HCoV-229E showed symptoms of a cold including headache, sneezing, malaise and sore throat with fever and cough.
Later in 1967, HCoV-OC43 was separated from successive passages and from organ enrichment in the mit's brain.
Clinical characteristics of HCoV-OC43 infection appear similar to infections generated by HCoV-229E which are figuratively indiscretiosible from influenza A virus and other respiratory tract pathogens like rhinovirus.
Both HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are spread all over the world and they are likely to be prominently transmitted during the winter with temperate weather.
Typically, the gradual growth period of these two viruses is less than a week, followed by about 2-weeds.
According to a study by human volunteers, healthy individuals infected with HCoV-229E developed a mild cold.
Severe lower respiratory tract infection appears in few patients who are incapable of immunity.
SARS, also known as “aptical pneumonia”, was the first well-docuted epidemic in human history due to HCO and the factor for this, the third HCO discovered is SARS-CoV.
The first case of SARS can be identified at the end of 2002 in Guangdong province of China.
The SARS epidemic reported 8,096 cases with 774 deaths, spreading to many countries and continents.
In addition to the highest spreaders, it was estimated that nearly two secondary cases could increase due to every case with a gradual development period of 4 to 7 days and the maximum viral bearing appeared on the 10th day of the disease.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially show symptoms such as muscular pain, headache, fever, malaise, and chills, followed by breathing pain, coughing, and respiratory crishes.
Lymphopenia, neurotic liver work tests and increased creatine kinage are common abnormalities related to the laboratory of SARS.
Circulating aerocellular damage, epithelial cell proliferation and increased macrophages are also seen in patients with SARS.
Approximately 20-30% of patients need intensive care and mechanical ventilation later.
In addition to the lower respiratory tract, usually many organs, including gastrointestinal tract, liver and kidneys with cytokeine storm, can also be infected in these severe cases, which can be fatal, especially for patients with an incompetent immunity.
For the first time, the virus was separated from an open biopsy of the address patient who traveled from Gwangzhu to Hong Kong.
Since then, a lot of efforts have been made in HCoV research.
HCoV-NL63 was separated from a 7-month-old child at the end of 2004 in the Netherlands.
It was initially found to spread to young children, the elderly, and incapable of immunity with respiratory diseases.
It is common in diseases caused by HCoV-NL63 to appear cold, conjunctivitis, fever, and bronchiolitis.
In the second independent study, an 8-month-old boy suffering from pneumonia described the isolation of the same virus from a nasal sample of an 8-month-old boy suffering from pneumonia.
Although it has been identified in the Netherlands, in fact it is spread all over the world.
It is estimated that HCoV-NL63 causes about 4.7% of common respiratory diseases and its extreme incidence occurs during early summers, spring, and winters.
HCoV-NL63 relates to obstructive larynx-inflammatory, also known as croups.
In the same year, HCoV-HKU1 was separated from the 71-year-old man who was hospitalized with pneumonia and bronchiolitis in Hong Kong.
In addition to pneumonia and bronchiolitis, HCoV-HKU1 was also reported to be related to severe asthma outbreaks.
Like HCoV-NL63, HCoV-2229E and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 was found all over the world, causing mild respiratory diseases.
HCoVs, all of these four communities, have become well adapted to humans and are usually less likely to mutuated to cause excessive pathogenic diseases, although accidents have occurred due to unknown reasons as in a rare case of a more toxic sub-type of HCoV-NL63, which has recently been reported to cause severe lower respiratory tract infections in China.
Usually, they also become less toxic or pathogenic when these HCoVs acquire the ability to be efficiently transmitted and to maintain themselves continuously in humans.
MERS-CoV was first isolated from the lungs of a 60-year-old patient in Saudi Arabia in 2012, developing acute pneumonia and kidney failure.
Where most laboratory confirmed cases originate from the Middle-East, cases imported with occasional secondary dissemination in close relatives have been reported in many European countries and Tunisia.
The second secondary outbreak took place with 186 confirmed cases in South Korea in 2015.
Clinical manifestations of MERS characterized by progressive severe pneumonia correspond to SARS.
Different from SARS, acute kidney failure also develops in many patients with MERS, which has so far been seen only in MERS in diseases caused by HCoV.
More than 30% of patients show gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhea and vomiting.
As of February 14, 2020, more than 2500 laboratory confirmed cases were reported with a high mortality rate of 34.4%, making MERS-CoV one of the most destructive viruses known to humans.
From mid-December 2019 to the end, herds of pneumonia patients related to SARS-CoV-2 infection were found in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
The World Health Organization (WHO) declared the ongoing outbreak of lower respiratory tract infection due to SARS-CoV-2 as an international worrying public health emergency and also named the disease COVID-19.
As of March 3, 2020, 90,053 cases have been confirmed across the world with an untreated death rate of 3.4%.
Remarkably, Hubei China's case fatality rate is 4.2%, compared to 1.2% outside of it.
SARS-CoV-2 causes severe respiratory infections like SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV that appear as a fever, cough, and respiratory distress.
Diarrhoea is also seen in some patients.
Pneumonia is one of the most severe symptoms and can rapidly aggravate as acute respiratory distress syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very similar due to high nucleotide sequence of 82%, they fall in different branches of the racetic lineage.
SARS-CoV-2 is clearly less pathogenic, but more transmissible than SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Asymptomatic patients infected with SARS-CoV-2 have been reported and can contribute to its rapid prevalence around the world.
Comparing with the other six HCoVs of SARS-CoV-2 and seeing asymmetry has shown very interesting similarities and variations.
First, the recovery period of the HCoV disease and the duration of the procedure are very similar.
In this regard, SARS-CoV-2 follows the general trend of other six HCoVs.
Secondly, the severity of symptoms of COVID-19 is similar to SARS-CoV and HCoV (i.e. HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
On the one han hand, an infection of SARS-CoV-2 shows characteristics that are commonly seen during infections of HCoVs acquired from the community, including non-exclusive, miligratory or no symptoms.
On the other hand, a small subset of severe cases of COVID-19 can also be seen, as in the case of SARS-CoV infection, although the ratio is slightly low.
Thirdly, the transmission of SARS-CoV-2 is also characterized by variants characterized by both HCoVs and SARS-CoV acquired from the community.
On the one hand, the infectivity of SARS-CoV-2 is as high as the infectivity of HCoVs acquired from the least community.
On the other hand, it is yet to be verified whether the infectivity of SARS-CoV-2 decreases after advancing in humans, such as SARS-COV and MERS-COV.
Finally, like other HCoVs, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in feces samples.
Whether the retal-oriented transmission of SARS-CoV-2 plays an important role in at least certain conditions like SARS-CoT, is yet to be clarified by future studies.
It's also very interesting to see if the SARS-CoV-2 community can behave seasonally like cases of HCoVs.
Nevertheless, the characteristics of SARS-CoV-2, including contagiousness, pathogenisity and persisting prevalence after going into humans, will be influencing on the final outcome of the ongoing COVID-19 outbreak.
HCoVs, achieved from all four communities causing mild symptoms, have become well adapted to humans.
From another point of view, it may also be true that humans have been well adapted to these four HCoVs.
In other words, both can be surviving from ancient HCoV epidemics.
The HCoVs that cause serious diseases in humans and humans who develop serious HCoV diseases have been eliminated.
For this to happen, the accumulation of customized mutations preventing nutrient restriction factors will have to replicate HCoVs in humans in sufficient limits.
In this sense, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak persists and the more incrucates people, the more likely it is to be fully adapted to humans.
If it is well adapted, its transmission in humans will be harder to prevent by quarantine or other infection control measures.
For many years, CoVs acquired from four communities are being transmitted to the human population while generating colds in incapable of immunity.
These viruses do not need animal cells.
On the contrary, highly pathogenic SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV have not been well adapted to humans and their transmission in humans cannot be maintained.
They need to stay and spread in their acquisitive trench and probably find a chance to be transmitted to receptive human goals by one or more intermediate and growing nutrients.
SARS-CoV-2 characteristics are similar to both SARS-CoV/MERS-COCO and HCoVs acquired from four communities.
At least in the present time it is very contagious like HCoVs acquired from the community.
However, it is more pathogenous and less pathogenic than SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV derived from the community.
It remains to be seen whether it will be fully adapted to humans and will be transmitted to humans without any cells or intermediate animal nutrients.
It will be very useful to discuss the definitions and characteristics of the gradual evolution of HCoVs, natural, koshes, intermediates, and enhancing nutrients, before discussing the animal origins of HCoVs.
An animal is a nutrient with a gradual growth of HCoV, if it shelters a very close ancestor with sharing higher similarities at nucleotide sequential levels.
The ancestral virus is usually well-optimated and non-diaplogant in this nutrient.
In the same way, the cells provide HCoV for a consistent and long-term shelter.
In both conditions, trouces are naturally infected and have natural nutrients of HCoV or its ancestral virus.
Conversely, if HCoV enters a new intermediate nutrient just before entering or around humans, it is not well adapted to new nutrients and is often pathogenic.
It can act as an azuctive source of an intermediate nutrient human infection and can play the role of an increased nutruin by temporarily replacing the virus and then transmitting it into humans to increase the level of infection.
If a HCoV cannot maintain its transmission within the intermediate nutrient, it can go into an end-infection.
On the other hand, HCoVs can also be adapted towards intermediate nutrient and can maintain locality for a long time.
In this case, the intermediate nutrient becomes a natrient.
