An alternative proposal favors recombination between Pangolin SARS-CoV-2-related beta-CoV and RaTG13 in third wild animal species.
As the motivational force of successive growth, recombination is widely present in beta-CoVs.
The immediate origin of SARS-CoV-2 is still not known.
In addition to highly pathogenic HCoVs, the zoonic origin of HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-HKU1 have also been studied.
Jatiological evidence indicated that both HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-229E may have originated from bat CoVs, while HCoV-OC43 and HCoV-HKU1's ancestral virus have been found in Mooshak.
It has been reported that the bat named ArCoV.2 (Applachen Ridge CoV), which was found in a three-colored bat of the North U.S., saw a close association with HCoV-NL63.
On the other hand, HCoV-229E Hipposideros/GhanaKwam/19/2008 was genetically related to another bat CoT, found in Ghana, although Kemlids are also its suspected intermediate nutrients.
