Alternatively, where bat alpha-Coots are the genes pool of HCoV-229E, alpaca and humped camels can be intermediate nutrients that transmit viruses to humans, just like the case of MERS-CoV.
MERS-CoV is an excellent example of interracial transmission to humans from humped camels and humped camels from bats.
The gradual development of MERS-CoV from bats is known at its primary identification and has become even stronger by subsequent findings.
It is clear that bats provide a rich range of virus species for interracial exchange and interracial transmission of genetic fractions.
Long life, dense settlements, near-social interactions and immense ability to fly are favorable conditions for all bats to be the ideal “virus broadcaster”.”
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has entered hummed camels for several decades.
It has become well adapted into these camels that have changed from intermediate nutrient to stable and natural cosh nutrient.
MERS-CoV causes a very mild disease and maintains a relatively low mutuation rate in these animals.
It is sporadic transmission accident in humans and humans remain the last nutrient of MERS-CoV because its transmission cannot be maintained.
Contrary to the role of camels in the transmission of MERS-CoV-2, the role of pangolin in the transmission of SARS-CoV-2, if any, is different.
Specifically, pangolin beta-CoVs is much more pathogentic in pangolins.
They can be the last nutrient of SARS-CoV-2-related beta-Covs, like civets in the case of SARS-CoV.
Many possibilities of interracial transmission of SARS-CoV-2 from animals to humans have to be accepted or denied in future studies.
First, bats can have cells of almost identical SARS-CoV-2-related virus to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans can share ecological housing with bats by killing or mining of coal.
Second, pangolin can be one of intermediate-enhancing nutrients in which the SARS-CoV-2-related virus was newly entered.
Human beings become infected with the virus by killing and consuming game meat.
It is possible that many mammals, including pets, can be receptive to SARS-CoV-2.
There is a need for a survey of antibodies in domestic and wild animals.
Thirdly, as mentioned above, the recombination and adaptation of SARS-CoV-2 may have occurred in the third species that are in contact with both bats and pangolins.
The animal origin of SARS-CoV-2 is still being discovered.
In addition to a variety of animal nutrients, three key factors in relation to viral are also important in easily transcending the species barriers of CoV.
First of all, their relatively higher mutuation rate in RNA replication.
