Nevertheless, the mutuation rate of CoVs is one million times higher thas their nutrients.
At the same time, the mutation rate is often higher when the CoVs are not well adapted to the nutrient.
Compared to SARS-CoV with a high mutuation rate, the mutuation rate of SARS-CoV-2 is clearly low, which points to a higher level of adaptation in humans.
Probably, it has already adapted to the second nutrient closer to humans.
With SARS-CoV-2, it also applies to MERS-CoV which has been well adapted into hummed camels.
Theoretically, it is unlikely that the genetic flow will rapidly deactivate vaccines and antivirals against SARS-CoV-2.
Second, the large RNA in CoV puts additional plasticity on genome mutation and genome changes for recombination, increasing the chances of interracial co-receiving, which is beneficial for the emergence of novel CoVs if the conditions are appropriate.
