The effect of the pandemic and its mortality varies for men and women.
Studies conducted in China and Italy have a higher mortality rate in men.
The highest risk for men is in their 50's, and the interval between men and women is only low at the age of 90.
In China, the mortality rate for men was 2.8 percent and for women was 1.7 percent.
The exact reasons for this gender-distination are not known, but genetic and behavioral factors can be a cause.
Gender-based immunological differences, reduced prevalence of smoking in women and developing co-morbid conditions in men such as high blood pressure at a younger age compared to women can contribute to high mortality rates in men.
In Europe, 57% of the infected individuals were male and 72% of those who died of COVID-19 were men.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government is not keeping an eye on gender-related statistics of COVID-19 infections.
Research has shown that viral diseases like Ebola, HIV, influenza, and SARS affect men and women differently.
A high percentage of healthcare workers, especially nurses, are women, and they are more likely to be exposed to the virus.
The World Health Organization (WHO) announced on February 11, 2020 that the disease's official name would be "COVID-19."
Tedros Adhanom Gebreyesus, head of WHO, pointed out that C.O. stands for Coronavirus, VI means Virus, D's Mean Diseases, and the meaning of 19 year when the outbreak was first identified i.e. December 31, 2019.
This name was chosen to avoid references to a specific geographical location (such as China), species of animals or a group of people who are in line with international recommendations for nomenclature aimed at preventing humiliation. The virus that generates COVID-19 is called severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
The WHO additionally uses the "COVID-19 virus" and the "virus responsible for COVID-19" in public communications.
Both the disease and the virus are generally referred to as "coronavirus."
During the initial outbreak in Wuhan, China, the virus and the disease were commonly called "Coronavirus" and the "Wuhan coronavirus."
In January 2020, the WHO recommended 2019-nCov and 2019-nCoV acute respiratory disease as interim names for the virus and disease, which compliates 2015 guidelines for not using places in disease and virus names.
Formal names of COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were released on 11 February 2020.
Due to the capacity limitations in the standard supply chains, some digital manufacturers are printing health materials such as nostal phosphate and parts of the ventilator.
In an example, when an Italian hospital urgently needed ventilate valves, and the supplier was unable to provide in the required time-frame, a local startup printed 100 valves required overnight by reverse-engineering.
After an early outbreak of COVID-19, conspiracies, misinformation and misconceptions about the origins of the disease, levels, prevention, treatment, and other aspects emerged and spread rapidly online.
Humans seem to be able to spread the virus in some other animals.
Studies failed to find evidence of viral replication in pigs, ducks, and chickens.
There is no medicine or vaccine approved for the treatment of the disease.
International research on vaccines and drugs in COVID-19 is being done by government organizations, academic groups and industry researchers.
In March, the World Health Organization launched "Solidarity Trials" to assess the healing effects of four existing antiviral compounds with the highest hope of efficacy.
There is no vaccine available at this time, but various agencies are actively developing vaccine candidates.
Previous work is being used on SARS-COCoV as both SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 use ACE2 receptors to enter human cells.
Three immunization strategies are being examined.
First of all, researchers aim to build a complete virus vaccine.
The goal of the use of such viruses, whether inactivated or dead, aims to generate an accelerated immune response of the human body towards a new infection with COVID-19.
The goal of a second-trapped vaccine is to create a vaccine that sensitizes the immune system for some sub-divisions of the virus.
In the case of SARS-CoV-2, such research focuses on S-contaminant proteins that help the virus embit into the ACE2 enzynzes.
A third strategy is of nucleic acid vaccines (DNA or RNA vaccines, a new technology of vaccine-making).
Experimental vaccines from any of these strategies will have to be tested for safety and efficacy. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of a vaccine began with four volunteers in Seattle.
The vaccine contains a harmless genetic code copied from the disease-causing virus. Antibody-dependent growth is said to be a potential challenge for vaccine development for SARS-COV-2, but it is controversial.
More than 300 active clinical trials are being carried out as of April 2020.
Seven tests were evaluating the already approved treatment for malaria, including four studies on hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Destined to be notified by the end of April, with nine Phase III trials on Remdesivir in many countries, working on re-purpose antiviral drugs in most Chinese research.
As of April 2020, a dynamic review of clinical developments for COVID-19 vaccines and drug candidates was underway. Several existing antiviral drugs are being evaluated for the treatment of COVID-19, including remdesivir, chloroquine and hydroxychloriquine, Lopinavir/Ritonavir combined with Lopinavir/Ritonavir with Interferon beta.
As of March 2020, experimental evidence is available for the efficacy of Remdesivir.
Clinical improvement was observed in patients treated with non-approvated use of remdesivir.
Phase III clinical trials are being conducted in the US, China, and Italy. Chloroquine, which is already used for the treatment of malaria, was studied in February 2020 with preliminary results in China.
However, the equivalent of the research has been called for a review.
Korean and Chinese health authorities recommend the use of chloroquine.
However, the Wuhan Institute of Virology recommended a daily dosage of one gram, noting that twice the same dose is extremely dangerous and can be life-threatening.
On 28 March 2020, the FDA issued an emergency use permission for hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine at the discretion of physicians treating people with COVID-19. The guidelines of the Chinese 7th version also include Interferon, ribavirin or Umifenovir for use for protection from COVID-19.
Early statistics indicate that high doses of ribavirin are necessary for the adjustment of artificial ambient SARS-CoV-2.
Nitazoxanide has been recommended for further studies in organisms after reflecting low-concentrators of SARS-CoV-2. Studies have shown that the initial thorn protein priming by transmembrane Protease Serine 2 (TMPRSS2) is necessary for the penetration of SARS-CoV-2 by intraction with ACE2 refrigerator.
There are major limitations of studies of chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine without azithromycin, which have prevented the medical community from adopting these treatments without further studies. Osltamivir does not respect artificial ambient SARS-CoV-2 and has no known role in COVID-19 treatment.
Increasing cytokine amounts in the later stages of severe COVID-19 can be a complication.
There is evidence that hydroxychloroquines may have properties that prevent the increase in cytokine amounts. Talkilizumab has been included in the treatment guidelines by China's National Health Commission after a short study was completed.
It's going through phase 2 non-randified trials at the national level in Italy after showing positive results in people with severe disease.
To identify the amount of cytokine combined with serum ferritin blood test, it aims to withstand such enhancements, which are believed to be the cause of death in some affected people.
Interlukin-6 receptive antide was approved by the FDA based on retrospective case studies for the treatment of steroid refractive cytokine delimination syndrome induced by CAR T cell therapy, in 2017.
To date there is no randomized, controlled evidence that Tosilizumab is an effective treatment for CRS.
Pure and concentrages produced by the immune system of people recovering from COVID-19 are being screened as a non-vaccinated method of inactivated immunization.
This strategy was used for SARS with inconclusive results.
Viral neutralization is the anticipated mechanism, by which inactive antibody therapy can mediate protection from SARS-CoV-2.
Although other activities, such as antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and/or phagocytosis may be possible.
Other forms of inactive antibody therapy, for e.m. using manufactured monoclonal antibodies, are in development.
